# Untitled

Dual MOFs composites: MIL-53 coated with amorphous UiO-66 for enhanced photocatalytic oxidation of tetracycline and methylene blue
Xuesheng Liu\(^{1}\), Xiangyu Zhao\(^{1}\), Hong Meng\(^{2}\) (✉), and Junsu Jin\(^{1}\) (✉)
\(^{1}\) Beijing Key Laboratory of Membrane Science and Technology, Beijing University of Chemical Technology, Beijing 100029, China  \(^{2}\) State Key Laboratory of Chemistry and Utilization of Carbon Based Energy Resources, College of Chemistry, Xinjiang University, Urumqi 830046, China
© Tsinghua University Press 2022
## ABSTRACT
In this work, we proposed a novel strategy for the photocatalytic degradation of the target pollutants tetracycline (TC) and methylene blue (MB) using core–shell dual metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) composites. A series of mesoporous composites MIL-53@UiO-66 were synthesized by solvent-thermal synthesis via coating UiO-66 on the surface of MIL-53. The results show that under the same degradation conditions, only 30 and 15 min are needed to degrade 93% of TC and 96% of MB in the photo-Fenton reaction system, respectively. The amorphous shell layer brings stronger adsorption to the catalyst. MIL-53@UiO-66 composites with equalizing Fermi level are formed to promote photon absorption and electron transfer. Meanwhile, the MIL-53@UiO-66 composites with excellent stability will be a promising catalyst for environmental remediation.
## KEYWORDS
dual metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), core–shell structure, tetracycline, methylene blue, photocatalysis
## 1 Introduction
With the continuous development and progress of human society, the problem of environmental pollution is becoming more and more serious. In particular, the problem of water pollution has an overwhelming influence on the destruction of nature and mankind health [1–3]. It is imperative to search for a feasible and efficient water pollution control technology. Photocatalysis technology has been widely concerned depended on its strongpoints such as nominal cost, green environmental protection, and good purification effect [4, 5]. Over the last few years, researches on photocatalytic degradation of pollutants have been widely reported. However, photocatalysis technology still faces great challenges in practical application, such as low utilization ratio of visible light and accelerated recombination of photogenerated carriers.
Lately, metal-organic framework materials (MOFs) have been widely concerned as a new material. MOFs, also considered to be porous coordination polymers (PCPs), were assembled by the interconnection of inorganic nodes and organic ligands [6, 7]. Similar to metal oxides, MOFs can also absorb light energy to produce electron–hole pairs, many MOFs, such as MIL-53, MIL88A, UiO-66, and ZIF-8, were synthesized and used in the field of photocatalysis [8–10]. Among them, MIL-53(Fe), as an iron-based MOF, was synthesized from ferric chloride and terephthalic acid [11]. MIL-53(Fe) can absorb visible light more widely than other MOFs, which has been widely studied because it is pollutionfree and cost-effective [12–14]. However, compared with inorganic semiconductors, the conductivity and visible light
response ability of MIL-53 are still insufficient. So far, researchers have tried to prepare numerous MIL-53 composites to enhance its photocatalytic activity, such as MIL-53(Fe)@TiO\(_{2}\) [15], MIL-53(Fe)/PDI [16], and BiOI/MIL-53(Fe) [17]. Recently, an interesting MOF-on-MOF hybrid design has received special attention as an approach to create a hybrid MOFs by integrating two or more different types of MOFs. The method required the grow of the guest MOFs on the basis of the synthesized host MOFs. Usually, the composition and properties of the guest MOFs are different from those of the host, and the hybrid MOFs have excess properties compared with the original material [18, 19]. The Zr–O bond in UiO-66 (Zr) provides hydrothermal stability, and zirconia nodes have been shown to have unprecedented catalytic performance and large specific surface area, enabling UiO-66 to play a role in the adsorption and degradation of pollutants [20, 21]. However, its application in the field of visible light is limited because of its wide band gap. Therefore, UiO-66 is usually used to recombine with narrow band gap materials such as MIL-88B-on-UiO-66 and UiO-66@ZIF-8 [22, 23].
Herein, we successfully designed a core–shell MIL-53@UiO-66 dual MOFs photocatalyst by coating UiO-66 on the surface of MIL-53 assisted by surfactants. Due to the lower conduction band of UiO-66, the transition electrons excited by visible light in MIL-53 can be quickly transferred to UiO-66, which effectively suppresses the recombination of hole electron pairs. In addition, the introduction of UiO-66 greatly increases the specific surface area of the catalyst, enhances the adsorption capacity of pollutants,
and exposes more active sites. In visible light \((\lambda>420\;\mathrm{nm})\), MIL-53@UiO-66 composites showed greater performance for the photocatalytic oxidation of tetracycline (TC) and methylene blue (MB) than that of pristine MIL-53 and UiO-66. Furthermore, the amorphous UiO-66 shell has made a great contribution to the stability of the composite. Our conclusion provides a benefit method for the preparation of a novel MOFs-based photocatalyst.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Materials
## 2.2 Preparation of samples
## 2.2.1 Synthesis of MIL-53
As previously reported, MIL-53 was prepared by a solvothermal method [24]. \(10\ \mathrm{mmol\ MeCl_{3}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}\) and \(10\ \mathrm{mmol\ H_{2}BDC}\) were dissolved in \(50\,\mathrm{\mL}\) N,N-dimethyl formamide (DMF), then the solution was ultrasounded for \(30\ \mathrm{\min}\). The solution was transferred into a \(100\ \mathrm{~mL}\) hydrothermal kettle and the temperature was kept at \(150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24~\mathrm{h}\). After the solution was cooled down to room temperature, the powder from the autoclave reactor was collected. Then the powder was washed three times with DMF and methanol respectively, and finally dried in a vacuum drying oven at \(60\,^{\circ}\!C\) for \(12\,\mathrm{{h}}\).
## 2.2.2 Synthesis of MIL-53@UiO-66
MIL-53@UiO-66 was prepared by a solvothermal method. \(0.5\ \mathrm{mmol}\) \(\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}\) and \(0.5\ \mathrm{mmol}\) \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}\) were added into \(30~\mathrm{mL}\) DMF and then stirred for \(30~\mathrm{\min}\) to dissolve completely. Following \(198\ \mathrm{~mg~}\) MIL-53 and \(100~\mathrm{~mg~}\) cetyl trimethyl ammonium bromide (CTAB) were dissolved in the mixture accompanying ultrasound for \(30\ \mathrm{min}\). The resulting mixture was put into a \(100~\mathrm{mL}\) hydrothermal autoclave and kept at \(120~^{\circ}\!C\) for \(24~\mathrm{h}\). After cooling down to room temperature, the powder was washed with DMF and methanol for three times and finally dried in vacuum at \(60\,^{\circ}\!C\) for \(12~\mathrm{{h}}\). By changing the amount of MIL-53, the obtained product was expressed as MIL-53/UiO-66- \(x\) \((x=0.5,\) 0.7, and 1.0), where \(x\) is the mass ratio of UiO-66 to MIL-53. UiO-66 was prepared without adding MIL-53 by using the above process.
## 2.3 Characterization method
Details of characterization method are available in the ESM.
## 2.4 Photocatalytic activity test
The performances of TC and MB photodegradation under visible light were studied by a multi-site photochemical reaction instrument (CEL-LAB500) (Fig. S1 in the ESM). \(5\;\mathrm{mg}\) Sample was dispersed in \(30\;\mathrm{mL}\) TC \((20\;\mathrm{ppm})\) and MB \((10\;\mathrm{ppm})\) solution and
stirred for \(30~\mathrm{\min}\) in the dark to reach the equilibrium of adsorption and desorption. After that, the xenon lamp light source \((500\;\mathrm{W})\) with a \(420\ \mathrm{nm}\) filter was turned on and the surface light intensity was determined by an optical power meter to be \(140\ \mathrm{\mW/cm}^{2}\). A certain amount of solution was taken to centrifuge at set intervals of time. The relative concentration of supernatant was detected at 357 and \(664\;\mathrm{nm}\) by ultraviolet–visible (UV–vis) spectrophotometer, respectively. As in the above steps, \(0.01\mathrm{~M~H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) was added to the photo-Fenton reaction after the dark reaction, and \(0.5~\mathrm{g/L}\) PS was added to the photoactivated persulfate reaction after adsorption and desorption equilibrium.
In addition, the stability of the composite was tested through 4 repeated experiments. TC was used as the degraded pollutant, and the test process was similar to the photocatalytic activity test process above. The catalyst after each test was obtained by filtration, and the adsorbed TC was cleaned and removed by deionized water and methanol repeatedly and dried in vacuum at \(60~^{\circ}\!C\) for \(12~\mathrm{{h}}\). The obtained catalyst was then used for the next test reaction.
In order to determine the main active substances of MIL-53@UiO-66- \(x\) in the process of photocatalytic degradation, MIL-53@UiO-66-0.7 was selected to carry out free radical trapping experiments. TC as the degraded pollutant, BQ, EDTA-2Na, and IPA were added to the solution to capture superoxide radical \((\cdot\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-})\), hole \((\mathfrak{h}^{+})\), and hydroxyl radical (-OH) [25, 26].
## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1 Characterization
The crystal structure of prepared catalysts was studied by X-ray diffractometer (XRD) (Fig. 1(a)). The XRD diffraction peaks of the synthesized MIL-53 and UiO-66 powders are consistent with those reported in the past [27–29]. MIL-53@UiO-66 composites have the characteristic peaks of MIL-53 and UiO-66. However, the intensity of the main characteristic diffraction peaks of MIL-53 has changed obviously in MIL-53@UiO-66, and a few of the characteristic peaks shifted or weakened. This phenomenon indicates that the introduction of UiO-66 changes the interaction between metal and organic ligands, thus changing the crystal structure of MIL-53 [5]. Furthermore, with the augment of the specific gravity of UiO-66, the intensity of the characteristic peak belonging to UiO-66 at \(7.4^{\circ}\) gradually increases, and tends to cover the adjacent diffraction peak belonging to MIL-53, which may be attributed to the fact that the surfactant advances the nucleation barrier of UiO-66 so as to inhibit its nucleation to form amorphous. XRD results show that the composites are composed of MIL-53 and UiO-66.
The types of chemical bonds on the surface of different samples were proved by Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectroscopy. In Fig. 1(b), UiO-66 has similar peaks near 552, 670, 748, 1,405, 1,585, and \(1,660\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), which belong to \(\mathrm{Zr-O}_{2}\), O–C–O
symmetrical extension, and \( \mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O} \) extension of \( \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BTC} \) ligands in UiO-66, respectively [30, 31]. Two strong absorption peaks at 1,531 and \( 1,384~\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1} \) can be seen in the spectrum of MIL-53, which are ascribed to the asymmetric vibration of carboxyl group \( \nu_{\mathrm{as}} \) \( \scriptstyle(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O}) \) ) and symmetrical vibration \( \nu_{\mathrm{a}} \) \( \scriptstyle(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O}) \) ), respectively. The bands at 1,627 and \( 751~\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1} \) are identified as the \( \mathrm{C}=\mathrm{C} \) stretching vibration and \( \mathrm{C}-\mathrm{H} \) bending vibration of benzene ring in the organic bond, respectively. And the peak at \( 538~\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1} \) is attributed to the stretching vibration of Fe–O bond [32, 33]. In addition, the wide absorption band between 2,500 and \( 3,500~\mathrm{cm}^{-1} \) is related to the \( -\mathrm{OH} \) bending and stretching vibration of water [34]. Obviously, MIL-53@UiO-66 composites have all the characteristic peaks belonging to MIL-53 and UiO-66, which proves that the composites were synthesized successfully.
Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM) were used to reveal the morphological changes of materials before and after composition (Fig. 2). MIL-53 is an irregular polyhedron with good crystallinity, which is similar to previous reports [35, 36]. UiO-66 presents a reunited cube structure [37]. Noticeably, the surface of MIL-53 has a rough transformation after composition (Fig. 2(c)), which is consistent with the change of XRD. Combined with the element distribution of TEM, the highly uniform distribution of \( \mathrm{Zr} \) on the surface of the composite provides very close evidence for the recombination of MIL-53 and UiO-66 particles (Figs. 2(h) and 2(i)). It can be clearly observed that amorphous UiO-66 has been coated to MIL-53 epitaxy (Fig. 2(f)), which is helpful to enhance the stability and adsorption capacity [38, 39]. \( \mathrm{N}_{2} \) adsorption–desorption isotherms were used to analyze the specific surface area, pore volume, and pore size of bare MIL-53, UiO-66, and MIL-53@UiO-66-0.7. The results are shown in Table S1 in the ESM. As shown in Fig. S4(a) in the ESM, all samples boast adsorption–desorption isotherms of type IV and hysteresis loops of type H3, indicating the existence of mesoporous structure in materials, which is consistent with the result of the pore size distribution map in Fig. S4(b) in the
ESM [40, 41]. The specific surface area and pore volume of MIL-53 are \( 3.49\,\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g} \) and \( 0.008\,\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g} \), respectively, while MIL-53@UiO-66-0.7 has a larger specific surface area \( (404.71\,\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}) \) and pore volume \( (0.438\,\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}) \). However, the specific surface area of MIL-53@UiO-66-0.7 composite is lower than that of pure UiO-66 \( (855.96\,\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}) \), which may be due to the introduction of MIL-53 covered part of the UiO-66 porosity. Generally, higher specific surface area would be conducive to the leakage of more active sites, which promotes the transfer of photogenerated carriers in the photocatalyst and also assists the adsorption of pollutant molecules on its surface [42, 43].
The surface chemical state of the samples was analyzed by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS), and the results are shown in Fig. S5 in the ESM and Fig. 3. In Fig.S5 in the ESM, MIL-53@UiO-66-0.7 contains C, O, Fe, and Zr elements determined by the survey spectrum, which demonstrates the existence of MIL-53 and UiO-66 in the composite. The peaks of Fe \( 2\mathrm{p}_{1/2} \) and Fe \( 2\mathrm{p}_{3/2} \) shift from 725.56 and \( 711.50~\mathrm{eV} \) to 726.37 and \( 712.07~\mathrm{eV} \), respectively (Fig. 3(a)), and the peaks of Zr \( 3\mathrm{d}_{3/2} \) and Zr \( 3\mathrm{d}_{5/2} \) move from 185.12 and \( 182.77~\mathrm{eV} \) to 185.07 and \( 182.71~\mathrm{eV} \), which is lower compared with original UiO-66 (Fig. 3(b)). This result shows the electronegativity change of metal ions, which could be understood as the enhanced chemical bond between UiO-66 and MIL-53 [44, 45]. XPS results further confirm the close combination of UiO-66 and MIL-53.
## 3.2 Photocatalytic degradation performance
In this work, TC and MB were selected as the representatives of organic pollutants, and the catalytic activity of MIL-53@UiO-66-x dual MOFs composite photocatalysts in visible light, photo-Fenton, and light-assisted persulfate oxidation was investigated. The adsorption experimental data are shown in Fig.S6 in the ESM, from which we can see that all materials can reach the equilibrium of adsorption and desorption after \( 30\,\mathrm{min} \). Figures 4(a)–4(c) display the result of TC degradation experiment. It could
be seen in Fig. 4(a) that the degradation rate of pure MIL-53 in \(120\,\mathrm{\min}\) is only \(30\%\) . After introducing UiO-66, the effect is obviously improved which may be owing to the fact that the photoexcited electron further transfers to UiO-66. Among them, the highest degradation rate of MIL-53@UiO-66-0.7 is \(90\%\) . The results of photo-Fenton reaction experiment (Fig. 4(b)) show that the degradation rate of pure MIL-53 after \(30\;\mathrm{min}\) is \(80\%\) , and the highest efficiency of the composite is \(93\%\) . Similarly, in the lightassisted persulfate oxidative degradation experiment (Fig. 4(c)), the highest degradation efficiency of MIL-53@UiO-66-0.7 is \(90\%\) under visible light irradiation for \(50\;\mathrm{min}\) , while that of pure MIL53 is \(85\%\) . Analogically, Figs. 4(d)–4(f) are the result of MB degradation experiment. The degradation efficiency of pure MIL53 in visible light, photo-Fenton, and light-assisted persulfate oxidation is \(41\%\) in \(120\ \mathrm{min}\) , \(38\%\) in \(15~\mathrm{min}\) , and \(78\%\) in \(50\;\mathrm{min}\) , respectively. After compounding with UiO-66, the efficiency is significantly improved, and the highest is \(91\%\) in \(120\;\mathrm{min}\) , \(94\%\) in \(15\;\mathrm{min}\) , and \(96\%\) in \(50\;\mathrm{min}\) , correspondingly. It can be concluded that the order of photocatalytic degradation efficiency is photoFenton reaction \(>\) photo-assisted persulfate oxidation \(>\) visible light degradation. The effect of \(\mathrm{pH}\) change on degradation was investigated and the results are shown in Fig. S7 in the ESM. Under the strong acid condition \(\mathrm{(pH}=3)\) , the removal rates of TC and MB decrease significantly. In the \(\mathrm{pH}\) range of 5–11, the removal rates have no significant change, which are all higher than \(80\%\) within \(120\ \mathrm{min}\) , indicating the stability of the material over a wide \(\mathrm{pH}\) range. TOC removal was used to evaluate the mineralization degree of TC and MB (Fig. S8 in the ESM). The results further prove that both of them have a high degree of mineralization in aqueous solution, which is \(72.8\%\) and \(90.7\%\) ,
respectively. In addition, the recycling performance of MIL\(53@\)UiO-66-0.7 was verified by repeated experiments of four cycles. With TC as the degradation product (Fig. S9 in the ESM), the removal rate of the catalyst remains largely unchanged after four cycles, which further proves the photocatalytic stability of the MIL-53@UiO-66-0.7 composite. From the X-ray diffraction patterns, and SEM and TEM images (Figs. S9(b)–S9(d) in the ESM), it could be seen that the structure of MIL-53@UiO-66-0.7 remains stable after four cycles, which also confirms the high stability and reusability of MIL-53@UiO-66-0.7.
## 3.3 Optical and electronic properties
The optical properties of the samples were evaluated by UV–vis diffuse reflectance spectroscopy (UV–vis DRS). As we can see in Fig. 5(a), UiO-66 has poor absorption performance in the visible light range. MIL-53 and MIL-53@UiO-66-0.7 have better absorption abilities, and the absorption of MIL-53@UiO-66-0.7 is stronger than that of MIL-53 in the range of \(500{-}650\ \mathrm{nm}\) . The results indicate that the enhanced light absorption ability of MIL\(53@\)UiO-66-0.7 is beneficial to the photocatalytic efficiency. For understanding the possible electron transfer mechanism, it is necessary to confirm the valence band and conduction band positions of the catalysts. With extrapolating the linear part of \((\alpha h\nu)^{2}\) vs. curve, the bandgap widths of MIL-53 and UiO-66 could be estimated as 2.85 and \(4.00~\mathrm{eV}\) , respectively (Fig. 5(b)) [46, 47]. Their flat-band potential \((E_{\mathrm{FB}})\) was determined by Mott–Schottky (M–S) diagram (Fig. 5(c)). The curves of MIL-53 and UiO-66 have positive slopes, indicating that they are n-type semiconductors [42, 48]. The \(E_{\mathrm{FB}}\) values of MIL-53 and UiO-66 are \(-0.39\) and \(-0.32\;\mathrm{eV}\) (vs. \(\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}\) at \(101=7\) ) respectively. In
general, the flat-band potential of n-type semiconductor is \(0.2\mathrm{~V~}\) more positive than the CB position, so the CB potential of MIL-53 and UiO-66 are \(-0.59\) and \(-0.52\mathrm{~eV}\) (vs. \(\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}\) at \(\mathrm{pH}=7\)), respectively. The \(E_{\mathrm{CB}}\) values of MIL-53 and UiO-66 are \(-0.39\) and \(-0.32\mathrm{~eV}\) (vs. NHE), respectively. The \(E_{\mathrm{VB}}\) values of MIL-53 and UiO-66 are 2.46 and \(3.68\mathrm{~eV}\) (vs. NHE), respectively. In addition to light absorption, photoelectron–hole transport and separation are also important factors affecting photocatalytic performance. Electron and hole lifetime extensions were evaluated by photocurrent response and electrochemical impedance experiments. From Fig. 5(e), it can be detected that the photocurrent density of MIL-53@UiO-66-0.7 is still 10 times that of MIL-53 and UiO-66 after five on-off lamp tests at \(10\;\mathrm{s}\) intervals. This further confirms that MIL-53@UiO-66-0.7 composite can effectively inhibit photoelectron–hole recombination [49, 50]. The electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) experiment also authenticates this result. In Fig. 5(f), the radius of MIL-53@UiO-66-0.7 is significantly smaller than that of pure MIL-53 and UiO-66, verifying that MIL-53@UiO-66-0.7 has lower resistance and higher charge separation efficiency [51, 52]. Similarly, PL spectra suggest that MIL-53@UiO-66-0.7 had the longest photogenerated carrier lifetime (Fig. S10 in the ESM).
## 3.4 Possible photocatalytic mechanism
The main active substances in photocatalytic degradation system were determined by free radical capture experiment. TC aqueous solution was added with BQ, EDTA-2NA, and IPA to capture \(\cdot\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}\), \(\mathrm{h}^{+}\), and \(\cdot\mathrm{OH}\). Both EDTA-2Na and IPA inhibited the degradation efficiency of the photocatalytic system in Fig. 6(a), testifying that hole \((\mathrm{h}^{+})\) and hydroxyl radical (·OH) are the key active species in the photocatalytic system. For further confirming the existence of free radicals in the photocatalytic system of MIL-53@UiO-66-0.7 composite, ESR was used to detect the free radicals. In Fig. 6(b), DMPO- ·OH and DMPO- \(\cdot\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}\) signals were not detected under dark conditions, and typical DMPO- ·OH signal was observed in the spectrum after illumination for a period of time whose intensity is higher than pure MIL-53 (Fig. S11 in
the ESM), while the characteristic peak of DMPO- \(\cdot\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}\) is still not obvious, which proves that ·OH was generated in the photocatalytic reaction process but no \(\cdot\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}\) radical was generated [53, 54]. Sequentially, we speculated that the possible electron transfer mechanism of MIL-53@UiO-66-0.7 composite under visible light irradiation, as shown in Fig. 7. Exposing to light, MIL-53 is excited and the electrons transition from the valence band to the conduction band. The electrons in the conduction band then migrate to the conduction band of UiO-66. Because the CB potential of UiO-66 \((-0.32\mathrm{~eV}\) vs. NHE), is corrected by standard potential of \(\cdot\mathrm{O}_{2}/\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}\) \((-0.33\mathrm{~eV}\) vs. NHE), the production of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}\) is thermodynamically infeasible. The holes generated in the valence band of MIL-53 are maintained at the valence band of MIL-53 and further oxidized water to produce hydroxyl radical (·OH). In photo-Fenton system, the Fe(III)-O cluster of catalyst MIL-53@UiO-66-0.7 can decompose \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) to produce ·OH radical (Eqs. (3) and (4)). The persulfate in the coupling process can be activated not only by Fe(III) in MIL-53 (Fe) through Eqs. (6) and (7), but also by photoelectron through Eq. (5).
\[
\mathrm{MIL-53@UiO-66-0.7}+i\nu\rightarrow\mathrm{h}^{+}+\mathrm{e}^{-}
\]
\[
\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}+\mathrm{h}^+\rightarrow\cdot\mathrm{OH}+\mathrm{H}^+
\]
\[
\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}+\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}_2\rightarrow\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}+\mathrm{HO}_2+\mathrm{H}^+
\]
\[
\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}+\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}_2\rightarrow\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}+\cdot\mathrm{OH}+\mathrm{OH}^-
\]
\[
\mathrm{e}^{-}+\mathrm{S}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{8}^{2-}\rightarrow\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{-}+\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}
\]
\[
\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}+\mathrm{S}_2\mathrm{O}_8^{2-}\rightarrow\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}+\mathrm{S}_2\mathrm{O}_8^{2-}
\]
\[
\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}+\mathrm{S}_2\mathrm{O}_8^{2-}\rightarrow\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}+\mathrm{SO}_4^{2-}+\mathrm{SO}_4^{-}
\]
## 4 Conclusions
In conclusion, a range of core–shell MIL-53@UiO-66 composites
were triumphantly synthesized by uncomplicated solvothermal approach. Compared with bare MIL-53 material, MIL-53@UiO-66 composites show better catalytic performance for the degradation of tetracycline and methylene blue in either visible light irradiation, photo-Fenton system or photoactivated persulfate system. Among them, the optimal MIL-53@UiO-66-0.7 has the highest transient photocurrent response and the lowest electrochemical impedance. It is confirmed that \( \mathrm{h^{+}} \) and ·OH are the major active species in photocatalysis, while \( \cdot\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-} \) plays a relatively small role in photocatalysis. MIL-53@UiO-66 composites have significant photocatalytic performance, which is due to the increase of specific surface area, the improvement of visible light absorption range, and the enhancement of photoinduced electron hole pair separation efficiency. The mechanism analysis shows that the charge transfer of MIL-53@UiO-66 composites is carried out through the interface of MIL-53 to UiO-66, which is from the CB of MIL-53 to the CB of UiO-66. In addition, the prepared MIL-53@UiO-66 photocatalysts have excellent stability and reusability, which further indicates that the system has a great application prospect in wastewater treatment.
